WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
 - So now we're going to move from merely matching to matching and extracting.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.240
 So we're going to say, hey, I would like to not only have you take this template,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.360
 this little pattern, the string pattern, regular expression pattern,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.400
 run it across the line, I want you to give me all the ones that match.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.680
 And I want a list of those.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.760
 And that's what we're going to use the find all.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.800
 So search gives a true false, find all gives a list of all the strings that match.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.080
 So if there's four of them, you'll get four things in the list.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.720
 If there's nothing that matches, you'll get an empty list.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.840
 So let's take a look at what we got going here.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.960
 So instead of calling search, we call find all.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.480
 We still pass in the string that we're looking through.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.560
 And then we have our little template pattern.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.160
 And this is a new bit of regular expression.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.920
 Any little bracket operation, square bracket, is one character.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.080
 That's just a character.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.560
 But then in between here is a set of allowed characters.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:01:00.280
 So 0-9 means a single digit, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, or 9.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.280
 But that's really one character.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.440
 And then we have, so that's one character.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.480
 And then when the plus applies to that, which means if we look at this whole thing,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.280
 this whole thing says one or more digits.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.440
 That's the code we write in a regular expression that says one or more digits.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.240
 And we're just going to use that in our regular expression by itself.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.680
 So we're going to look for any string that's one or more digits.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.440
 And pull it out and give it back to me.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.520
 So it's going to look.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.600
 So that's my little template, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.800
 Oop, got it.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.120
 Stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.120
 Oop, got it.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.960
 Stamp, stamp, stamp, stamp, got it.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.720
 So what we get back after we ask find all to find all of the one or more digit

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.520
 strings is 2, 9, and 42.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.560
 So it actually parsed it.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.120
 It split it.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.880
 It found all these things and said, I found them all for you.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.760
 And here they are, 2, 19, and 42.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.360
 So it's a list of three strings because that's how many it found.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.720
 Now, it might have found none.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.360
 And we would have got an empty list at that point.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.600
 But it found some.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:02.320
 OK.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.280
 So just as an example, we did this thing.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.800
 We get 2, 19, and 42.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.920
 But if I said this, that basically is an uppercase vowel, A, E, I, O, or U.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.120
 So that's one letter.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.160
 And that's one or more.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:25.680
 So it's saying something like A, A would match, E, I would match, O, O would match.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.280
 But if you look now, it's saying, OK, I'm looking for one or more.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.640
 Minimum one or more uppercase, A, E, I, O, U is a set of characters, one or more

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.400
 uppercase letters.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.040
 And so it's like, look, there's an uppercase.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.960
 But it's an M. No, no, no, no, no, uppercase, no uppercase, no uppercase,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:46.160
 no uppercase, found nothing, did not find anything.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.800
 And so it gives us back an empty list.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.600
 And so it's like, find all the things that match this.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.080
 And the answer is, none matched, here's your list of nothing.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.040
 And so you have to check.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.680
 That's how you have to check even if you got something, because it's not

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.720
 going to return you false.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.160
 It returns you a list with no items in it.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.360
 Now, the way it works, like I said, it sort of is taking this template and

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.080
 stamping it across the line, stamping across the characters.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:19.000
 Now, there's a behavior that might not be intuitive you at the very beginning.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.880
 But the notion of what we call greedy matching.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:26.360
 And that is, when it can match more than one possible string,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.280
 overlapping string, it chooses the largest of the overlapping strings.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.080
 And so the easiest way is to show this with an example.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.320
 And we're saying, I want something that starts with an F with one or

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.120
 more characters and ends with a colon.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.600
 So that's my little stamp, that's my template.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.400
 So it starts with an F, good, that's good.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.120
 One or more characters, dah, dah, dah, dah, dah, have a colon.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.720
 So that could be from colon, that would match.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.920
 But look, I've got another colon here, and this is just continuing on with one or

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.680
 more characters, and this.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:01.160
 So the question is, do we get this, or do we get this part, right?

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.600
 And the answer is, with greedy matching, is we get the larger of the two, okay?

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.880
 And so what you get back is somewhat counterintuitive.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.280
 You get the whole thing as the match, from colon using the.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.480
 We could have got from colon, but the reason it picks this is this one's longer.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.400
 So any time it has a choice, it picks the longer one.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.360
 And that's what greedy is, meaning it's probably better described as larger or

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.960
 tending toward the longest string, or something like that.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.640
 So you can, of course, suppress this behavior, like everything.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.280
 In programming regular expressions, you simply add another character.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.800
 And so now, it's going to say, I would like to start with letter f,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.840
 any character, one or more times, and then this question mark,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.800
 this is still one little thing, non-greedy, okay?

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.840
 And so that just says, do it not greedy,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.400
 which just means that it prefers the shorter of the strings.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.160
 And so now, it could still match this string or this string.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.880
 But because it's been told to not be greedy, it chooses this string instead, and

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.040
 that's the string that we get.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.760
 And so that's the not greedy, and

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.080
 you just had the question mark after the asterisk.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.600
 So it's usually an asterisk question mark or a plus question mark.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.600
 That's a two thing, that's zero more characters non-greedy, and

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.200
 that's one or more characters non-greedy.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.280
 Actually, most of the time, it seems to me that the non-greedy would be

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.560
 the more reasonable default, but that's not how it is.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.480
 Greedy is the default, and non-greedy is optional.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:35.640
 Now, we can play some more with this stuff, okay?

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.360
 And so let's take a look at this little example,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.000
 where we have a non-blank characters, backslash capital S.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.000
 One or more of those non-blank characters, followed by an at sign, and

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.200
 then again, one or more non-blank characters.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.480
 So this is looking for strings that have an at sign with non-blank characters on

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.480
 both sides.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.840
 This is an example of where it sort of comes to this at, and it goes this way, and

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.120
 it does it in a greedy manner.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.600
 If you told it to not be greedy, it would give you this, these three characters.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.600
 But we're telling it to go greedy, so it goes all the way to here, and

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.020
 stops at this blank, and then stops at this blank.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.400
 And so that's a nice little thing.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:18.080
 Find the at signs, go to the first blank, blank, and pull that stuff out.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.320
 And so that, with one little match, you've pulled this thing out.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.240
 Now, of course, we've done that before with other techniques.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.000
 So that's just another way to pull stuff out.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.960
 Now, if we, we get this whole thing, but

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.360
 what if that's not exactly what we wanted?

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.600
 We can tell, we can, we, we can give it

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.840
 a matching string that's different than the extracting string by adding parentheses.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.320
 And so here's another example, where we basically say, this is our string.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.820
 We want to match from at the beginning, followed by a space, followed by,

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.120
 ignore the parentheses for the minute, one or more non-blank characters,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:05.280
 followed by an at sign, followed by one or more non-blank characters.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.720
 So this is also going to, if there's no from,

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:09.720
 it's not going to be looking for that, right?

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.720
 So it demands the from is here, so it matches that, and

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.320
 the space is demanded as well, and then it says, oh, non-blank characters, great.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.840
 I got an at sign, great, non-blank characters, oops, stop there.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.240
 And so this is what's going to match.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.800
 Now, the key is that we don't actually want that back in our extraction.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.680
 What we really want back in our extraction is this part right here.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.160
 So what we do is we put parentheses in.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.400
 Parentheses don't, are code, they're, they're code in the regular expression world.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.660
 Parentheses say, start your extraction and end your extraction.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:45.760
 And so when you do this with a parentheses, when you, when you do it,

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.040
 you know, without a parentheses, you get, you get the whole from, right?

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.520
 Without a parentheses, oh wait, no, okay, that, that doesn't have a from in it.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:01.320
 So, but if you do that with the parentheses, the, you match the from, but

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.520
 you only get the, this bit to come out as well.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.760
 So you can add this to make the matching part more precise, but

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:11.980
 without changing what you get returned, and

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:15.360
 you specify what you want to get returned with the parentheses.

